---
import LegalLayout from "@/layouts/LegalLayout.astro";
import { legal, subprocessors } from "@/lib/legal";

const legalMailto = `mailto:${legal.legalEmail}`;
---

<LegalLayout
  title="Data Processing Agreement"
  description={`Art. 28 GDPR Data Processing Agreement (AVV) for ${legal.brandName}.`}
  heading="Data Processing Agreement"
  eyebrow="AVV · Art. 28 GDPR"
>
  <p>
    This Data Processing Agreement (“DPA”), including its Annexes, forms part of
    the <a href="/legal/terms">Terms of Service</a> between the Merchant
    (“Controller”) and <strong>{legal.legalName}</strong> (“Processor”,
    “DeclineGuard”) under Article 28 of the GDPR. It applies when DeclineGuard
    processes personal data of the Controller’s end customers (and similar data
    subjects) in providing the Service.
  </p>
  <p>
    By creating an account, connecting a Lemon Squeezy store, or otherwise using
    processing features, Controller agrees to this DPA. If you need a signed PDF
    copy, email <a href={legalMailto}>{legal.legalEmail}</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Definitions</h2>
  <p>
    Terms used here have the meaning in the GDPR. “Services” means DeclineGuard
    payment-recovery and related features. “Customer Personal Data” means
    personal data relating to Controller’s end customers (and other data
    subjects whose data Controller submits) processed by Processor on
    Controller’s behalf.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Subject matter, duration, nature &amp; purpose</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Subject matter:</strong> processing Customer Personal Data to operate recovery detection, storage, dashboarding, and recovery email sequences.</li>
    <li><strong>Duration:</strong> for the term of the Services agreement and until deletion/return under this DPA.</li>
    <li><strong>Nature:</strong> collection via webhooks/API, storage, organisation, transmission (email), retrieval, restriction, erasure.</li>
    <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> solely to provide the Services instructed by Controller; not for Processor’s own marketing to end customers.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>3. Types of personal data &amp; data subjects</h2>
  <h3>3.1 Data subjects</h3>
  <p>
    End customers / subscribers of Controller (and any other individuals whose
    data appears in Lemon Squeezy webhook payloads Controller enables).
  </p>
  <h3>3.2 Types of data</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Identity &amp; contact: name, email address</li>
    <li>Commercial: product/plan name, amount, currency</li>
    <li>Identifiers: subscription, invoice, event / store identifiers</li>
    <li>Service metadata: update-payment URL, email send timestamps, recovery status, test-mode flags</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Special categories (Art. 9) are not required for the Service; Controller
    shall not intentionally submit special-category data.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Instructions</h2>
  <p>
    Processor shall process Customer Personal Data only on documented
    instructions from Controller, including in the Terms, this DPA, and product
    configuration (connected stores, sequences, brand settings), unless required
    by EU or Member State law. If Processor believes an instruction infringes
    GDPR, Processor will inform Controller without undue delay.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Confidentiality</h2>
  <p>
    Persons authorised to process Customer Personal Data are bound by
    confidentiality (contractual or statutory) and receive access only as
    needed.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Security (Art. 32)</h2>
  <p>
    Processor implements appropriate technical and organisational measures,
    including but not limited to:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Encryption in transit (TLS)</li>
    <li>Encryption of Lemon Squeezy API keys at rest</li>
    <li>Access control &amp; authentication</li>
    <li>Least-privilege internal access</li>
    <li>Logical separation of customer data in the application database</li>
    <li>Rate limiting and abuse protections</li>
    <li>Backup / resilience measures as provided by infrastructure vendors</li>
    <li>Incident response procedures</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Details may be updated as measures improve, without reducing the overall
    security level.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Subprocessors</h2>
  <p>
    Controller authorises Processor to engage subprocessors. Current
    subprocessors relevant to Customer Personal Data include:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Subprocessor</th>
        <th>Role</th>
        <th>Location</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {
        subprocessors.map((s) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{s.name}</td>
            <td>{s.purpose}</td>
            <td>{s.location}</td>
          </tr>
        ))
      }
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Processor will impose data-protection obligations on subprocessors
    materially no less protective than this DPA. Processor remains responsible
    for subprocessors’ performance. Processor will notify Controller of intended
    additions or replacements of subprocessors (e.g. via email, website list, or
    in-app notice) at least <strong>14 days</strong> in advance where
    practicable. Controller may object on reasonable data-protection grounds; if
    unresolved, Controller may terminate the affected Services as its sole
    remedy.
  </p>

  <h2>8. International transfers</h2>
  <p>
    Where Customer Personal Data is transferred outside the EEA, Processor
    ensures Chapter V GDPR safeguards (e.g. adequacy or SCCs) via its
    subprocessors’ mechanisms. Controller authorises such transfers subject to
    those safeguards.
  </p>

  <h2>9. Assistance with data-subject rights</h2>
  <p>
    Taking into account the nature of processing, Processor shall assist
    Controller by appropriate technical and organisational measures, insofar as
    possible, for the fulfilment of Controller’s obligation to respond to
    requests to exercise data-subject rights. Controller remains responsible for
    responding to end customers. Processor will not respond directly to end
    customers as controller, except to redirect them to Controller or as
    required by law.
  </p>

  <h2>10. Assistance with security, DPIA &amp; prior consultation</h2>
  <p>
    Processor shall assist Controller with Art. 32–36 obligations, considering
    the nature of processing and information available to Processor, including
    relevant information for DPIAs regarding the Services.
  </p>

  <h2>11. Personal data breach</h2>
  <p>
    Processor shall notify Controller without undue delay after becoming aware
    of a personal data breach affecting Customer Personal Data, and provide
    information reasonably available to help Controller meet Arts. 33–34. Notice
    to: Controller’s account email and/or
    <strong>[MERCHANT SECURITY CONTACT — as provided in account]</strong>.
  </p>

  <h2>12. Return or deletion</h2>
  <p>
    Upon termination of Services or upon Controller’s written request, Processor
    shall, at Controller’s choice, delete or return Customer Personal Data and
    delete existing copies, unless EU or Member State law requires storage.
    Disconnecting a store in-product may trigger deletion of associated recovery
    records as implemented. Certifications of deletion may be provided upon
    reasonable request.
  </p>

  <h2>13. Audits</h2>
  <p>
    Processor shall make available information necessary to demonstrate
    compliance with Art. 28 and allow for audits by Controller or an authorised
    auditor, subject to: reasonable prior notice, confidentiality, no more than
    once per twelve months (unless a breach or competent authority requires
    otherwise), and preference for third-party audit reports / questionnaires
    before on-site inspection. Audits must not unreasonably disrupt operations
    or compromise other customers’ security.
  </p>

  <h2>14. Controller responsibilities</h2>
  <p>Controller warrants that:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>it has a valid legal basis to process and to instruct Processor to send recovery emails;</li>
    <li>its instructions are lawful;</li>
    <li>notices to data subjects (privacy policy, etc.) cover this processing;</li>
    <li>it will not configure the Service to process data unlawfully or beyond the Service’s intended scope.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>15. Liability</h2>
  <p>
    Liability under this DPA follows the liability provisions of the Terms,
    without prejudice to Arts. 82–84 GDPR and mandatory law.
  </p>

  <h2>16. Order of precedence</h2>
  <p>
    On data-processing subject matter, this DPA prevails over conflicting Terms.
    The GDPR prevails over both where applicable.
  </p>

  <h2>17. Governing law</h2>
  <p>
    This DPA is governed by {legal.governingLaw}, unless mandatory data-protection
    rules require otherwise.
  </p>

  <h2>Annex A — Technical &amp; organisational measures (summary)</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Access control (authentication, authorisation, least privilege)</li>
    <li>Encryption in transit; credential encryption at rest</li>
    <li>Secure development and dependency practices</li>
    <li>Logging &amp; monitoring of security-relevant events</li>
    <li>Vendor management for subprocessors</li>
    <li>Incident response &amp; breach notification workflow</li>
    <li>Availability / backups via infrastructure providers</li>
    <li>Staff confidentiality commitments</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Annex B — Contact</h2>
  <p class="meta">
    Processor: {legal.legalName}<br />
    {legal.streetAddress}, {legal.postalCode} {legal.city}, {legal.country}<br />
    Privacy: {legal.privacyEmail}<br />
    Legal: {legal.legalEmail}
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
